/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 341 0 R
/T (RFC 1035 \(Domain Names - Implementation and Specification\): Defines the strict structural format of DNS messages over UDP \(Port 53\). Understanding these fixed header boundaries is essential for implementing the bounded UDP/DNS packet parsing required by the eBPF verifier, allowing safe extraction of identifiers without requiring full semantic parsing. )
/E (RFC 1035 \(Domain Names - Implementation and Specification\): Defines the strict structural format of DNS messages over UDP \(Port 53\). Understanding these fixed header boundaries is essential for implementing the bounded UDP/DNS packet parsing required by the eBPF verifier, allowing safe extraction of identifiers without requiring full semantic parsing. )
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
128 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 342 0 R
/T (RFC 4732 \(Internet Denial-of-Service Considerations\): Outlines the architectural vulnerabilities of internet protocols to volumetric attacks. It establishes the foundational requirement for mitigation strategies to utilize both per-source and global rate-limiting mechanisms to preserve service availability during query floods. )
/E (RFC 4732 \(Internet Denial-of-Service Considerations\): Outlines the architectural vulnerabilities of internet protocols to volumetric attacks. It establishes the foundational requirement for mitigation strategies to utilize both per-source and global rate-limiting mechanisms to preserve service availability during query floods. )
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
129 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 343 0 R
/T (Netfilter vs. XDP Performance Studies: Existing literature on Linux networking performance highlights the limitations of standard nftables firewalls under high-volume load, specifically the CPU exhaustion caused by sk_buff memory allocation. In contrast, eBPF documentation and recent XDP proposals establish the network interface driver-level execution model as the modern standard for line-rate packet dropping and efficient DDoS mitigation. )
/E (Netfilter vs. XDP Performance Studies: Existing literature on Linux networking performance highlights the limitations of standard nftables firewalls under high-volume load, specifically the CPU exhaustion caused by sk_buff memory allocation. In contrast, eBPF documentation and recent XDP proposals establish the network interface driver-level execution model as the modern standard for line-rate packet dropping and efficient DDoS mitigation. )
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
